<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cayman Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1670 CE – present · capital George Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Three low islands south of Cuba, long a sleepy dependency of Jamaica, now a British Overseas Territory and one of the world's largest offshore financial centres.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> sighted the islands in 1503, and they were visited by turtle hunters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> for over a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded them, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1670">Treaty of Madrid</a> in 1670. Permanent settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-cayman">Grand Cayman</a> began only around the 1730s, with settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and the enslaved people they brought.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For most of their history the islands were a dependency of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, managed locally by a justice of the peace and, from 1831, an elected assembly of vestrymen. Islanders lived by turtling, shipbuilding, rope making and seafaring, and many men worked abroad as sailors. The Wreck of the Ten Sail in 1794 is a celebrated local tradition. Presbyterian missions shaped religious life.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> became independent in 1962, the Caymans chose to remain British and became a separate crown colony. Tax-neutral finance laws from the 1960s, tourism and diving transformed the economy, and the islands are today a prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-town-cayman-islands">George Town</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cayman-islands.jpg" alt="Cayman Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cayman_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1670 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>George Town</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cayman Islands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caymans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=cayman-islands" aria-label="Cayman Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2480 1463 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cayman Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2800 1719L4997 651M2800 1719L2917 1766"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2917 1766h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2800 1719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2800 1719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks George Town. <a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=cayman-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

